# Rotation pool — template. Copy to `.name-pool` and fill it in.
#
#   cp .name-pool.example .name-pool
#
# Ordinary human given names, used as placeholders in test fixtures. Every
# release, scripts/rotate-names.mjs swaps the ones currently in the tree for a
# fresh set drawn from here.
#
# WHY THE POOL IS GITIGNORED
# --------------------------
# Publishing the pool would defeat the rotation. Anyone could diff the names
# in a release against the pool, and whatever failed to appear in it would be
# the name that was never a placeholder.
#
# RULES FOR ENTRIES (all enforced, not conventions)
# -------------------------------------------------
#  - Ordinary given names, from a spread of origins. The point is that a real
#    name should be indistinguishable from a placeholder, which only works if
#    the placeholders look like real names.
#  - Never a name from .private-names. Rejected at load with an error.
#  - Never a name that already occurs anywhere in the tracked tree. Rotation
#    is a word-boundary find-and-replace over source, so a name that collides
#    with an identifier or an English word would corrupt real code.
#    Verify with:  node scripts/rotate-names.mjs --check
#  - Avoid anything that doubles as code: Max, Min, Val, Mark, Will, Rose,
#    Grace, Ruby, Ada. These pass a casual read and break the build later.
#  - At least (number of slots x 2) entries, so no name has to repeat from
#    one release to the next. A name that survived a rotation would be the
#    one fixed point in a churning set — exactly what stands out.
#
# One name per line. `#` starts a comment.

# Firstname
# Firstname
